---
import DocsLayout from "../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";
import Callout from "../../components/docs/Callout.astro";
import LinkCards from "../../components/docs/LinkCards.astro";
import { docHref } from "../../data/docs";
---

<DocsLayout slug="threads">
  <section aria-labelledby="start">
    <h2 id="start">Start a thread</h2>
    <p>Start a thread for each piece of work you hand to an agent. Every thread keeps its own agent, model, mode, and folder, so several can work at once.</p>
    <ol class="docs-steps">
      <li>Click <strong>New thread</strong> at the top of the sidebar, or press <kbd>⌘N</kbd>. (On Windows and Linux, use <kbd>Ctrl</kbd> wherever this page shows <kbd>⌘</kbd>.)</li>
      <li>The page asks <strong>What should we work on in</strong> and names a project. Click the project's name to pick another one, or choose <strong>Add project</strong> at the bottom of the list.</li>
      <li>In the composer's bottom row, the branch button says where the thread works, such as <strong>Works in the local checkout on main</strong>. Click it to choose <strong>Local checkout</strong> for the repository as it is now, <strong>Worktree</strong> for a branch and folder of its own, or <strong>OpenOrc Cloud</strong> for a machine in the cloud that keeps working while your Mac sleeps. In the Workspace, <strong>Choose folder</strong> picks the folder instead. See <a href={docHref("cloud-threads")}>Cloud threads</a>.</li>
      <li>In the composer's bottom row, choose the agent and model, and the mode. For a Claude model, choose the Claude account in the same picker.</li>
      <li>Type your request and press <kbd>Enter</kbd>.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>Along the bottom of the page, a dot for each day of the past year shows how many turns the project's agents finished that day: the more turns, the larger and darker the dot. The line above counts the project's turns and steps, each step being one tool call such as a file read or a command. Point at a day to see its turns, steps, threads, and how they split between agents.</p>
    <p>After the first reply, a small model gives the thread a short title. To rename it, click the title in the thread's header.</p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="composer">
    <h2 id="composer">Write a message</h2>
    <div class="docs-table">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Input</th>
            <th scope="col">What it does</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td><kbd>Enter</kbd></td>
            <td>Sends the message.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><kbd>Shift+Enter</kbd></td>
            <td>Starts a new line.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><code>@</code></td>
            <td>Mentions a file from the project.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><code>/</code></td>
            <td>Lists commands and your agent's skills. In a thread, <code>/compact</code> folds the conversation so far into a summary and <code>/fork</code> continues in a new thread.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>+</strong>, paste, or drop</td>
            <td>Attaches files of up to 20 MB each. Images show as thumbnails.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>The microphone, or <kbd>⌘⇧D</kbd></td>
            <td>Dictates into the message. See <a href="#dictate">Dictate a message</a>.</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>The disc before the microphone shows how full the context is, when the agent reports it. Click it for the amount and <strong>Compact context</strong>, which uses native compaction for Codex, Claude Code, OpenCode, Oh My Pi, and Pi. For Cursor, Antigravity, and fx, it starts a fresh session with a short handover brief. Either can lose earlier detail; the brief preserves less than native compaction.</p>
    <p>The disc at the end of the model button shows how much of this week's allowance is left on the account your next message would use. Click it for every account's limits. See <a href={docHref("agents") + "#usage"}>Usage and allowances</a>.</p>
    <h3>Quote the conversation</h3>
    <p>Select words in a message of the conversation and a note field opens under them. Type a note, or leave it empty, and click <strong>Save</strong> or press <kbd>Enter</kbd>. The passage joins your message as a quote above the text, with its note under it. Click the quote to read the whole passage or change the note, and <strong>×</strong> to remove it.</p>
    <h3 id="dictate">Dictate a message</h3>
    <p>Click the microphone beside the send button, or press <kbd>⌘⇧D</kbd>, and speak. Your words are written into the message where the cursor was, as they are heard. The microphone becomes a check that finishes the recording, and <strong>Cancel</strong> or <kbd>Esc</kbd> takes the words back out. Sending while you dictate finishes the recording first, then sends.</p>
    <p>Dictation runs on this computer by default. The first time, the microphone offers to download the speech model, Parakeet, which is about 731 MB and understands English; your voice stays on this computer. To transcribe with OpenAI on your own API key instead, see <a href={docHref("settings") + "#general"}>Settings</a>.</p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="while-working">
    <h2 id="while-working">Send while the agent works</h2>
    <p>
      You don't have to wait for a turn to end. Type while the agent works and press <kbd>Enter</kbd>: Codex, Claude Code, OpenCode, Oh My Pi, Pi, Antigravity, and fx read text messages during the current turn, so you can
      correct course without stopping them. The placeholder reads <strong>Say it during this turn…</strong>
    </p>
    <p>Cursor queues messages for the next turn. fx also queues images. A message waits after you switch the agent or change the mode, and while the conversation compacts.</p>
    <p>Once you type during a turn, the send button shows what <kbd>Enter</kbd> does: an arrow when the message goes into the turn, a pile when it queues. A second button beside it sends the other way, <strong>Queue</strong> or <strong>Send now</strong>, and so does <kbd>⌘Enter</kbd>. To hold a message for the next turn on purpose, click <strong>Queue</strong>.</p>
    <p>Queued messages wait above the composer as a pile that reads like <strong>2 queued</strong>, and go out one at a time, each as a new turn. Click the pile to list them. Each has <strong>Send now</strong>, which hands it to the running turn when the agent can take it, and <strong>×</strong> to remove it. Click a message's words to take it back into the composer. The queue survives a restart: OpenOrc sends what's left when it opens again.</p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="stop">
    <h2 id="stop">Stop a turn</h2>
    <p>While the agent works and the composer is empty, the send button becomes a stop button (<strong>Stop</strong>). Once you type, the send button comes back with a separate stop button beside it. Stopping ends the turn and denies any approval the agent was waiting for. The agent keeps its session, so your next message continues the conversation.</p>
    <Callout>
      <p>Stopping doesn't clear the queue. When the turn ends, the next queued message is sent, so remove queued messages first if you want the agent to stay idle.</p>
    </Callout>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="fork">
    <h2 id="fork">Fork a thread</h2>
    <p>A fork is a new thread that continues the conversation while the original stays as it is. Use it to try another approach without losing the first.</p>
    <p>Forking is available for Codex, Claude Code, OpenCode, Oh My Pi, and Pi. Cursor, Antigravity, and fx do not expose native conversation forks; start a new thread instead.</p>
    <p>To fork, choose <strong>Fork</strong> in the <strong>Thread actions</strong> menu (⋯) in the thread's header, type <code>/fork</code>, or hover your latest message and click <strong>Fork thread</strong>.</p>
    <p>The fork opens with the original title followed by "(fork)", and the same agent, model, and mode. It shares its parent's folder and branch, so both threads edit the same files.</p>
    <Callout>
      <p>A fork always takes the whole conversation. Its agent continues the original's session, which holds every turn, so there is no forking from an earlier message.</p>
    </Callout>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="side-by-side">
    <h2 id="side-by-side">Open threads side by side</h2>
    <p>You can show up to three threads at once, each in its own pane.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>To add a pane, choose <strong>Open beside</strong> in a sidebar thread's menu. <kbd>⌘</kbd>-clicking a thread or dragging it onto the open thread opens it in a tab of its own instead.</li>
      <li>Click inside a pane to focus it. Clicking a thread in the sidebar replaces the focused pane.</li>
      <li>Drag the divider between two panes to resize them, and double-click it to reset.</li>
      <li>To close a pane, click its <strong>Close pane</strong> button (×). <kbd>⌘\</kbd> closes every pane except the focused one.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>The panes share one side panel, which shows the thread whose controls opened it last. To give a thread a window of its own, choose <strong>Open in new window</strong> in its menu.</p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="sidebar">
    <h2 id="sidebar">The sidebar</h2>
    <p>At the top of the sidebar, after the window's buttons, are the button that hides the sidebar (<kbd>⌘B</kbd>), then <strong>Back</strong> and <strong>Forward</strong>, which step through the screens you've visited (<kbd>⌘[</kbd> and <kbd>⌘]</kbd>). With the sidebar hidden, the three lead the window's first header instead. Under them, <strong>New thread</strong> and the app's screens are rows: <strong>Tasks</strong>, <strong>Pull requests</strong>, and <strong>Automations</strong>. <strong>Tasks</strong> shows an amber count while tasks wait on you.</p>
    <p>Your threads follow under the list's name, <strong>My workspaces</strong>. The name is a menu that switches what the list holds: your own workspaces, an <a href={docHref("organizations")}>organization</a> you belong to, or your <a href={docHref("orclings")}>Orclings</a>. A dot on it says something waits behind it: an invitation, an unread chat or mention in an organization, or an Orcling. Beside the name are <strong>Search</strong>, which opens the command palette, <strong>Add project</strong>, and the filter.</p>
    <p>At the sidebar's foot are your picture, name, and plan once you sign in to <a href={docHref("cloud")}>OpenOrc Cloud</a>, or <strong>Sign in</strong>, then the update button while an update waits, and <strong>Settings</strong>.</p>
    <p>Each row is one line. A cube of dots leads it when there's something to report, then comes the thread's title. The cube moves while the agent works, and how it moves follows the work, such as reading, editing, or running a command. It is amber while the thread waits for your approval or your answer, red when it failed, and whole when it has a result you haven't read. A thread at rest with a pull request shows the pull request's state instead. A cloud thread also shows a cloud at the end of its row. Hover a row for when it last worked, where it works, and its branch.</p>
    <p>A project's name leads with the project's icon, or with an open cube when it has none. The cube's top fills while the project's threads work, and turns amber when one of them waits on you.</p>
    <p>Threads that an agent started, and the subagents an agent runs, sit under their parent thread. Click the chevron on the parent's row to fold or open them. See <a href={docHref("messaging")}>Threads working together</a>.</p>
    <h3>How the list is grouped</h3>
    <p>The list groups threads by project, under each project's name. Projects start the day ordered by their latest work, the most recent first, and hold that order until the next day, so work in one project doesn't move it past another. A project you add during the day goes to the top. Threads in the Workspace come last, under <strong>No project</strong>.</p>
    <p>Inside a project, threads are listed by the day they last worked, the most recent first: today's with no label, then <strong>Yesterday</strong>. A thread with threads under it goes by the latest work anywhere among them. While your pointer or the keyboard is in the list, rows hold their places; a row that would arrive, leave, or move does so shortly after you leave the list.</p>
    <div class="docs-table">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Group</th>
            <th scope="col">Holds</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Pinned</strong></td>
            <td>Pinned threads, always first.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>A project</td>
            <td>Its four latest threads and every other thread that's working or waiting on you, then <strong>Show N more</strong>. An older thread that still waits on you stays, under <strong>Earlier</strong>. The project's own <strong>Resolved</strong> ends its rows.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Resolved</strong></td>
            <td>The last row of each project: the project's threads you resolved, and those with no work since before yesterday.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Snoozed</strong></td>
            <td>Snoozed threads, until they wake. It follows the projects.</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>Click a project's name to fold or open its threads. A folded project still shows the thread you have open, and an amber count tells you how many of its threads wait on you. Point at the name for <strong>New thread</strong> in that project and <strong>More</strong>, which also has <strong>New task</strong>, <strong>Choose icon…</strong>, <strong>Project settings</strong>, and <strong>Remove project…</strong>.</p>
    <h3>Resolve a thread</h3>
    <p>Resolve a thread when you're done with it. Choose <strong>Resolve</strong> in its menu, press <kbd>⌘⇧A</kbd>, or drag it onto its project's <strong>Resolved</strong>. Its subthreads go with it, except pinned or snoozed ones. A thread that has seen no work since before yesterday moves there by itself, unless it's pinned, snoozed, or waiting on you.</p>
    <p>Click a project's <strong>Resolved</strong> to open it under the project's rows. To bring a thread back, choose <strong>Reopen</strong> in its menu, press <kbd>⌘⇧A</kbd>, or drag it out of Resolved onto the list. Sending a message to a resolved thread also reopens it. Reopening counts as work on the thread now, so it comes back as the first of its project's rows, with the subthreads that were resolved along with it.</p>
    <h3>Filter the list</h3>
    <p>The filter button beside the list's name has these choices:</p>
    <div class="docs-table">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Choice</th>
            <th scope="col">What it does</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>All threads</strong></td>
            <td>Lists every thread.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Pinned threads</strong></td>
            <td>Lists only pinned threads.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>A workspace's name</td>
            <td>Ticked, its threads are listed. Untick a workspace to leave it out of the list until you tick it again.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Import terminal sessions…</strong></td>
            <td>Continues sessions you started in a terminal. See <a href="#import">Import terminal sessions</a>.</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>To find a thread that isn't in view, or anything that was said in one, click <strong>Search</strong> or press <kbd>⌘K</kbd>.</p>
    <h3>Projects</h3>
    <p>Every project has its own group in the sidebar. On the New thread page, click the project's name in <strong>What should we work on in</strong> to start in another project, or choose <strong>Add project</strong> there or beside the list's name in the sidebar. To take a project off the list, point at its name in the sidebar, click <strong>More</strong>, and choose <strong>Remove project…</strong>. Its files, worktrees, and history are kept.</p>
    <h3>Thread actions</h3>
    <p>Right-click a thread, or open <strong>Thread actions</strong> (⋯) in its header. A right-click starts with <strong>New subthread</strong> and the ways to open the thread. Then comes where the thread works: its branch, marked <strong>Worktree</strong> or <strong>Checkout</strong>, then <strong>OpenOrc Cloud</strong> for a cloud thread. Point at either one for the moves from there. The header's menu starts with where the thread works and leaves out the choices that only apply to a thread you aren't looking at.</p>
    <div class="docs-table">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Action</th>
            <th scope="col">What it does</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>The branch</td>
            <td><strong>Show in Finder</strong> opens the thread's folder, <strong>Copy branch name</strong> copies the branch, and <strong>Move to a worktree</strong> or <strong>Move to the checkout</strong> moves a project thread and its uncommitted changes, and <strong>Remove worktree</strong> frees a worktree's folder and keeps its branch. A thread in the checkout shows the branch the checkout is on now. See <a href={docHref("git")}>Git and worktrees</a>.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>OpenOrc Cloud</strong></td>
            <td><strong>Move to Mac</strong> brings a cloud thread to this computer, and <strong>Open on Mac</strong> opens a read-only copy of its code. On a thread on this computer, the branch's row has <strong>Move to OpenOrc Cloud</strong>. See <a href={docHref("cloud-threads") + "#move"}>Move a thread between your Mac and the cloud</a>.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Open beside</strong></td>
            <td>Sidebar only. Opens the thread in another pane. See <a href="#side-by-side">Open threads side by side</a>.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Open in new window</strong></td>
            <td>Gives the thread a window of its own.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Open parent</strong></td>
            <td>Sidebar only. For a thread that sits under another, opens the parent. In the header, click the parent's name.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Pin</strong></td>
            <td>Keeps the thread in <strong>Pinned</strong> at the top of the list. <kbd>⌘⇧P</kbd></td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Snooze</strong></td>
            <td>Moves the thread to a folded <strong>Snoozed</strong> group until the time you pick, such as <strong>In 1 hour</strong>, <strong>Tomorrow morning</strong>, or <strong>Next week</strong>. A finished turn wakes it early, and so does <strong>Wake now</strong>.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Mark as unread</strong></td>
            <td>Sidebar only. Flags the thread for later. Opening it marks it read again. <kbd>⌘⇧U</kbd></td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>New subthread</strong></td>
            <td>Starts a thread under this one, in the same folder, with the same model and mode until you change them.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Fork</strong></td>
            <td>Continues the conversation in a new thread. See <a href="#fork">Fork a thread</a>.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Resolve</strong></td>
            <td>Moves the thread to <strong>Resolved</strong>, or back with <strong>Reopen</strong>. <kbd>⌘⇧A</kbd></td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Delete</strong></td>
            <td>Removes the conversation, and its worktree if it has one. Uncommitted work in the worktree is first committed to the thread's branch, which stays. Tasks the thread created stay too. Deleting a cloud thread deletes it from OpenOrc Cloud.</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p><kbd>⌘1</kbd> to <kbd>⌘9</kbd> open the first nine threads in the list, and <kbd>⌘⇧[</kbd> and <kbd>⌘⇧]</kbd> move to the previous or next one.</p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="import">
    <h2 id="import">Import terminal sessions</h2>
    <p>You can continue a Claude Code or Codex session that you started in a terminal.</p>
    <ol class="docs-steps">
      <li>Click the filter button beside the list's name in the sidebar and choose <strong>Import terminal sessions…</strong></li>
      <li>If you have more than one project, choose the one the session ran in.</li>
      <li>Check the sessions you want, or click <strong>Select all</strong>, then click <strong>Import</strong>.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>Each session becomes a thread that continues where the terminal left off. Sessions you already imported are marked <strong>imported</strong>.</p>
    <Callout>
      <p>OpenOrc looks for sessions in <code>~/.claude</code> and <code>~/.codex</code> only. Sessions kept elsewhere through <code>CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR</code> or <code>CODEX_HOME</code> don't appear.</p>
    </Callout>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="next-steps">
    <h2 id="next-steps">Next steps</h2>
    <LinkCards cards={[{ slug: "agents" }, { slug: "permissions" }, { slug: "work" }, { slug: "messaging" }]} />
  </section>
</DocsLayout>
